**Main Ideas**:
- The document explores the concept of "1.5-bit optimization" in various domains, comparing biological and computational systems.
- In DNA storage, Huffman-like encoding is used to achieve ~1.5 bits/base efficiency. Goldman et al. (2013) mapped ASCII text to DNA with variable-length codes, compressing 8-bit characters into 1.58 bits/base [GH-43A5E025468E L32].
- Biological systems, such as fungal growth, demonstrate similar optimization by converting substrate into structured biomass with efficiency >100% [GH-43A5E025468E L62].
- The 1.5-bit threshold is linked to thermodynamic efficiency, with DNA replication and neural coding operating near similar limits [GH-43A5E025468E L78].
- Computational methods, like 1.58-bit DNA-Aeon arithmetic coding, avoid error-prone homopolymers while maintaining efficiency [GH-43A5E025468E L32].

